window.docContents[28].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* There are two bodies now — whose mass is this?* Two particulars, Oscillator A and B, of one sort (harmonic oscillator); each black box contains one point-like body and its own wall spring. The oscillators do not interact. One name is one system; its records are states, configurations, or rival estimates to compare. R₁ and R₂ are distinct names even when parameters match. L2 opens each box into body and spring(s), and introduces their whole/part relations.* No new kinds: mass and stiffness share a rig-indexed oscillator bearer, allowing its k/m quotient.* B's mass can enter A's formula when only the kind is checked; one constant designation would collapse A and B into one system.* Stage 2: the kinded form carries its bearer.\n\nUnder L0's assumptions, each oscillator is a homogeneous, rigid, point-like body on one\nideal, massless spring attached to a fixed, infinitely massive wall, along a line. The\noscillator's mass is its body's mass, and its stiffness is its spring's. The oscillator\nis the whole characterized by ω₀, so k and m must carry the same oscillator index.\nThe two oscillators do not interact in L1. L2 revisits that assumption as it opens each\nbox into body and spring(s) and considers the pair as a whole.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies","header":"5.2.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Small-rigorous-improvement"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes--The-generated-indexes--Kinds-and-their-algebra":{"contents":"The column that earns this table is Algebra. A kind's authored edges are the complete statement\nof what may be done with it — multiplied by what, divided into what, raised to what power — because\nthe calculus infers none of them. A kind whose Algebra cell is empty supports no arithmetic at all,\nand being able to see that is the point.\n\n* Kind* Identity* Scale* Algebra* Theorems* attenExponent* AVS attenuation exponent* ratio* produced (1)* paramB · vegetationIndex → attenExponentconsumed (1)* transcendental : attenExponent → attenuationK* attenuation_roles_distinct* attenuationK* AVS vegetation attenuation* ratio* produced (1)* transcendental : attenExponent → attenuationKconsumed (3)* jacB · attenuationK → jacB* attenuationK · paramC → paramC* attenuationK · reflectivity → reflectivity* attenuation_roles_distinct* backscatter* AVS backscatter coefficient σ⁰* ratio* produced (2)* paramA · vegetationIndex → backscatter* paramC · reflectivity → backscatter* * jacB* AVS backscatter times NDVI* ratio* produced (2)* jacB · attenuationK → jacB* jacBPartial · reflectivity → jacB* * jacBPartial* AVS NDVI times gain c* ratio* produced (1)* vegetationIndex · paramC → jacBPartialconsumed (1)* jacBPartial · reflectivity → jacB* * paramA* AVS gain a (σ⁰ per NDVI)* ratio* consumed (1)* paramA · vegetationIndex → backscatter* * paramB* AVS rate b (inverse NDVI)* ratio* produced (1)* pureNumber · paramB → paramBconsumed (1)* paramB · vegetationIndex → attenExponent* * paramC* AVS gain c (σ⁰ per reflectivity)* ratio* produced (1)* attenuationK · paramC → paramCconsumed (2)* paramC · reflectivity → backscatter* vegetationIndex · paramC → jacBPartial* * pureNumber* AVS pure number* ratio* consumed (2)* pureNumber · paramB → paramB* pureNumber · vegetationIndex → vegetationIndex* attenuation_roles_distinct* reflectivity* AVS surface reflectivity* ratio* produced (1)* attenuationK · reflectivity → reflectivityconsumed (2)* paramC · reflectivity → backscatter* jacBPartial · reflectivity → jacB* * vegetationIndex* AVS vegetation index* ratio* produced (1)* pureNumber · vegetationIndex → vegetationIndexconsumed (3)* paramB · vegetationIndex → attenExponent* paramA · vegetationIndex → backscatter* vegetationIndex · paramC → jacBPartial* * length* length* ratio* produced (1)* speed · time → length* length_is_speed_times_time* speed* speed* ratio* consumed (1)* speed · time → length* length_is_speed_times_time* time* time* ratio* consumed (1)* speed · time → length* length_is_speed_times_time* massFraction* mass fraction* ratio* * * volumeFraction* volume fraction* ratio* produced (1)* volumeFraction ± volumeFraction → volumeFraction* * dl* 1* ratio* produced (2)* dl · dl → dl* transcendental : dl → dl* pk, tk* height* height* ratio* * * length* length* ratio* * * width* width* ratio* * * mass* mass* ratio* * mass_extensive* lengthK* length* ratio* * * massK* mass* ratio* * instAssemblesGridSMassK* length* length* ratio* * hLen* mass* mass* ratio* * hMass* lengthKind* length* ratio* produced (1)* lengthKind ± lengthKind → lengthKind* hLen* displacement* displacement* ratio* produced (2)* displacement ± displacement → displacement* factor · displacement → displacementconsumed (1)* displacement · displacement → displacementSq* doubled_east, hDisp, hScale, hSquare, vector_add_comm* displacementSq* displacement squared* ratio* produced (1)* displacement · displacement → displacementSq* hSquare* factor* scale factor* ratio* consumed (1)* factor · displacement → displacement* hScale* backscatter* backscatter coefficient* ratio* * * conductivityKind* ionic conductivity* ratio* * * gwc* gravimetric water content* ratio* * * ndvi* vegetation index* ratio* * * reflBistatic* reflectivity* ratio* * * reflMonostatic* reflectivity* ratio* * * reflectivity* reflectivity* ratio* * cholRowQ_magnitudes* refractiveIndexKind* refractive index (n)* ratio* * * relPermKind* relative permittivity* ratio* * * relaxTimeKind* dielectric relaxation time* ratio* * * vwc* volumetric water content* ratio* * * bloodGroup* abo-blood-group* nominal* * * length* length* ratio* * * mass* mass* ratio* * * mohsHardness* mohs-hardness* ordinal* * * bloodGroup* abo-blood-group* nominal* * * length* length* ratio* * * mass* mass* ratio* * * height* height* ratio* * * length* length* ratio* * * width* width* ratio* * \n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes\u0009The generated indexes","header":"33.10.1. Kinds and their algebra","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes--The-generated-indexes--Kinds-and-their-algebra"},"/#deployment-template":{"contents":"A model document says what a boundary is. A deployment document says what deploying one\nadds, and the two are different enough that a single document doing both does neither\nwell. The model's reader wants to know what is proved and at which carrier; the\ndeployment's reader wants to know what will run, on what, against which contract, and what\nwould have to fail for the result to be rejected.\n\nThe separation is not editorial tidiness. Almost everything the deployment adds is\nunproved by construction: an image is built, not derived; a shard size is measured, not\ntheorem-backed; a queue is chosen. Mixing that material into the model document lets the\nmodel's green nodes lend it an assurance it does not have. Keeping it separate forces the\ndeployment to carry its own evidence, which is what this template enumerates.\n\nThe template has 17 rubrics: 9 discharged by a section that states the answer, 2 by a view generated from the compiled environment, 3 by a kernel-checked declaration, and 3 by a labeled measurement.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"35. Deploying a domain model: the deployment template","id":"/#deployment-template"}});